  <!-- Stanford -->
  <article class="research-entry">
    <div class="research-date"><small>2026–Present</small></div>
    <div class="research-logo">
      <img src="{{ '/assets/img/stanford-university.png' | relative_url }}" alt="Stanford University" loading="lazy">
    </div>
    <div class="research-details">
      <h3>Research Assistant @ <a href="https://www.stanford.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Stanford University</a></h3>
      <p class="research-location">Stanford, California, USA</p>
      <p class="research-summary">
        Working with <a href="https://www.devo-evo.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Prof. Xiaojie Qiu</a> on single-cell and spatial
        omics to investigate cell-state transitions, tissue organization, and molecular programs underlying mammalian development and disease.
      </p>
      <ul class="research-bullets">
        <li>
          <span class="research-label">Human fetal heart development:</span> Analyzing PCW7 and 8 human fetal heart spatial transcriptomics data to
          characterize cardiac cell states, spatial niches, cell–cell communication, and 3D reconstruction during early heart development.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="research-label">3D embryonic development:</span> Building a 3D spatiotemporal atlas of mouse embryogenesis from E5.5–E7.5 to
          characterize cell-state transitions, spatial tissue organization, and developmental trajectories during early embryogenesis.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="research-label">Pediatric cardiomyopathy:</span> Leading a single-cell multi-omics and spatial transcriptomics study comparing
          myocardial states before and after ventricular assist device support (VAD) and between recovery and non-recovery to identify cellular
          programs, spatial niches, and potential biomarkers associated with myocardial recovery.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
